course: biology
lesson: 123
title: Bugs Bunny and Daffy Duck: real rabbits, hares and ducks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Bugs Bunny munches a carrot and asks «What's up, Doc?», but a real wild rabbit fills up on grass, clover and leafy plants, and carrots are only a small sugary treat.
explain: Bugs, with his long legs and life alone, is more like a jackrabbit, and jackrabbits are really hares: bigger, longer-eared runners that live above ground, while rabbits dive into burrows.
explain: Real rabbits are full of surprises: they thump a back foot to warn the warren, see almost all the way round, do happy twisting jumps called binkies, and even eat their own soft droppings to digest food twice.
explain: Daffy Duck shouts all day, but real ducks are mostly calm; oil from a gland near the tail keeps them waterproof, and a clever blood system stops their feet freezing on ice.
explain: At the park, ducklings follow their mother in a line, ducks tip upside down to dabble for food, and bread is the worst thing to throw them, while oats, peas or sweetcorn are much better.
fact: Wild rabbits mostly eat grass, clover and leafy plants; carrots are sugary and should be only a small treat for pet rabbits.
fact: Jackrabbits are hares; hares are bigger than rabbits, with longer ears and legs, and live above ground in nests in grass.
fact: Baby hares are born furry with open eyes and can hop soon after birth, while baby rabbits are born bare and blind underground.
fact: A rabbit thumps its back foot to warn other rabbits of danger, and the thud travels through the ground into the burrow.
fact: A rabbit's eyes are high on the sides of its head, so it can see almost all the way round, with only a small blind spot in front of its nose.
fact: A happy rabbit's jump-and-twist is called a binky, and it shows the rabbit feels safe and lively.
fact: Rabbits eat their soft droppings to digest their food a second time and get out vitamins missed the first time; their teeth never stop growing, and chewing tough grass all day wears them down.
fact: Daffy is a black duck, and the American black duck is a real bird; in mallards only the females give the loud classic «quack».
fact: Ducks spread oil from a gland near the tail over their feathers when they preen, so water rolls off, and dry down underneath traps warm air; thick spilled oil from a ship is different, as it clumps the feathers so water gets in, and an oiled duck gets cold and may sink.
fact: In a duck's legs, warm blood going down heats the cold blood coming back, so the feet stay just warm enough not to freeze on ice.
fact: Ducklings follow the first moving thing they see after hatching, usually their mother; this is called imprinting.
fact: Bread fills ducks with little goodness and rotting leftovers dirty the pond; oats, peas, sweetcorn or duck food are better.
fact: A duck that tips upside down with its tail up is dabbling, reaching for plants and small animals just below the surface; diving ducks go right under.
exercise: What do wild rabbits mostly eat? | Grass, clover and leaves | Carrots dug up from the farmers' fields | Nuts and berries from trees | Insects and wriggly worms
exercise: Bugs Bunny has long legs and lives alone. Which animal is he more like? | A hare, such as a jackrabbit | A guinea pig | A pet rabbit kept in a garden hutch | A red squirrel that lives in a tree
exercise: How are baby hares different from baby rabbits? | Hares are born furry with open eyes; rabbits are born bare and blind | Hares are born bare and blind; rabbits are born furry with open eyes | Both are born exactly the same | Hares hatch from eggs
exercise: Why does a rabbit thump its back foot on the ground? | To warn other rabbits of danger | To dig the start of a brand new burrow | To show it is hungry | To shake off mud
exercise: Why can a rabbit notice you even when it seems to look the other way? | Its eyes are high on the sides of its head and see almost all round | It has a third, tiny eye on the back of its head under the fur | It hears your heartbeat | It smells your shadow
exercise: Where is a rabbit's blind spot? | Right in front of its nose | Out to the left side, where its ear is | High up in the sky above its head | Far away on the horizon
exercise: What is a binky? | A happy jump with a twist that a relaxed rabbit does | A rabbit's warning thump | A soft nest of fur that a mother rabbit makes for her babies | A rabbit's sneeze
exercise: Why does a rabbit eat some of its own soft droppings? | To digest its food a second time and get more goodness | Because it is very ill and needs to see a vet straight away | To clean its hutch | Because it cannot find grass
exercise: Daffy Duck is always shouting. What are real ducks mostly like? | Mostly calm and quiet | Always noisy all day long | Silent, with no voice at all | Only noisy late at night
exercise: In mallards, who gives the loud classic «quack»? | The females | The male drakes | The ducklings | Both males and females equally
exercise: Why does water roll off a duck's back in beads? | It spreads oil from a gland near its tail over its feathers | Its feathers are made of a kind of plastic that never gets wet | Its skin is very hot | It shakes the water off in the air
exercise: Why don't a duck's feet freeze on ice? | Warm blood going down heats cold blood coming back | Ducks have no blood in their feet | Their feet are covered in thick fur | They stand on one foot at a time all day long
exercise: Why do ducklings follow their mother in a line? | They follow the first moving thing they saw after hatching | She ties them together in a line with long bits of pond grass | They are following her smell only | They are racing to the pond
exercise: What is a better food than bread to give park ducks? | Oats, peas or sweetcorn | Salty crisps and hot chips | Squares of milk chocolate | Old mouldy cake from the bin
exercise: A duck tips upside down with its tail in the air. What is it doing? | Dabbling for plants and small animals under the water | Trying to sleep | Drowning and needing someone to help it turn back up | Showing off to other ducks
exercise: What keeps a rabbit's ever-growing teeth the right length? | Chewing grass | Chewing small stones from the riverbank | Biting carrots only | Losing them each year
test: «my rabbit's fave food is carrots, he gets a whole bag a day» What is right? | Too much sugar; hay and grass first, carrots only as a small treat | Perfect, carrots are all a rabbit needs to stay healthy and strong | Fine, if they are washed | Fine, but peel them first
test: «4 rabbits in the hutch, how many ears is that» How many? | 8 | 4 | 6 | 16
test: «2 ducks and 3 rabbits on the lawn, how many legs» How many? | 16 | 20 | 10 | 14
test: «Bugs Bunny's a rabbit, it's in the name» What is right? | With long legs and a life alone above ground, he is more like a hare | Right, he is exactly like a pet rabbit, so the cartoon got it spot on | Right, rabbits always live alone | Right, all rabbits are called bunnies
test: «ew my rabbit's eating its poo, is it sick??» What is right? | It is normal; it digests its food twice to get more goodness | Yes, take it to the vet at once, it must have a tummy bug | Yes, it is starving and has nothing else to eat in its cage | Yes, it has lost its mind
test: What would happen to a duck caught in an oil spill? | The oil wrecks its waterproofing, so it gets cold and may sink | It would swim faster | It would stay warm and dry, as oil keeps water out of feathers | Nothing, oil helps ducks float
test: «chuck the ducks that old loaf, they love it» What is the best answer? | Bread is poor food and rots in the pond; give oats or peas | Fine, bread is a duck's natural food and the best thing for it | Fine, if you soak it in milk | Fine, as long as it is white bread
test: A scientist is the first moving thing some goslings see after hatching. What happens? | They follow the scientist around like a parent | They run away and hide from the scientist in the reeds | They ignore everyone | They fly south at once
test: «rabbit just jumped and twisted mid air, is it having a fit» What is right? | It is a happy binky; it feels safe and lively | Yes, call a vet at once | It is trying to scare you away from its cage | It has a sore back
test: In a cartoon, a duck's feet freeze into the ice and it is stuck. What is real? | Its warm-blood system stops its feet freezing to the ice | Ducks really get frozen in every winter | Ducks keep their feet warm in their pockets, as cartoons show | Ducks never stand on ice
test: «rabbit thumped the floor and the others legged it, why» What happened? | It was a danger warning, and they ran to safety | It was playing a drum game with the others | It wanted food | It was angry at the floor
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of the wild rabbit. | It subsists principally upon grasses, clover and leafy vegetation | It subsists principally upon carrots taken from cultivated fields | It subsists principally upon insects and earthworms | It subsists principally upon the fruits of trees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the jackrabbit? | It is a species of hare, larger and longer of limb than the rabbit, dwelling above ground | It is a domesticated rabbit which escaped from a farm and now lives in the wild | It is a species of rodent related to the squirrel | It is a rabbit which inhabits deep communal burrows
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the plumage of a duck sheds water. | Oil from a gland near the tail is distributed over the feathers during preening | The feathers are impregnated with a wax which the duck obtains from water plants | The elevated temperature of the skin evaporates the water as soon as it lands | The duck expels water from the feathers by vigorous flight
exam: Ascertain the process whereby newly hatched ducklings come to follow their mother. | Imprinting upon the first moving object perceived after hatching | Instruction by the father prior to hatching | Attraction exclusively to the colour of the mother's plumage | Physical attachment by means of vegetation
